WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:04.570
In the margins of old books there is a small drawn hand,

2
00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:06.760
a finger pointing at a line.

3
00:00:07.626 --> 00:00:10.586
Palaeographers call it a manicule.

4
00:00:10.626 --> 00:00:12.906
It doesn't explain anything.

5
00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:14.976
It says: here. Look.

6
00:00:16.836 --> 00:00:20.596
Borges imagined a library that held every possible book,

7
00:00:20.636 --> 00:00:23.366
and opened none of them at the right page.

8
00:00:24.078 --> 00:00:28.078
Ours fit in a pocket now, and they have started to behave like his.

9
00:00:28.118 --> 00:00:31.018
PDFs, scans, recordings, links.

10
00:00:31.058 --> 00:00:33.298
We know we read it somewhere.

11
00:00:33.338 --> 00:00:35.188
We can't find where.

12
00:00:38.366 --> 00:00:42.106
This is Scholaris. A hand that points to the page,

13
00:00:42.146 --> 00:00:43.426
then steps aside.

14
00:00:47.982 --> 00:00:50.392
It begins by letting go of the files.

15
00:00:51.245 --> 00:00:55.825
A comedia by Lope de Vega, printed in Seville around 1700.

16
00:00:55.865 --> 00:00:59.545
A recent paper on correcting old OCR.

17
00:00:59.585 --> 00:01:04.045
Nineteen minutes of Bécquer, read aloud for LibriVox.

18
00:01:08.057 --> 00:01:09.597
All at once.

19
00:01:10.605 --> 00:01:15.525
Each one goes to the press. The pages of the scan appear within seconds,

20
00:01:15.565 --> 00:01:18.515
and they can be read before the rest is finished.

21
00:01:19.915 --> 00:01:23.215
And a link: a short film about the manicule itself.

22
00:01:25.455 --> 00:01:28.955
Every scanned page is read by a model that looks at the image,

23
00:01:28.995 --> 00:01:33.485
not at an old text layer. Every recording is transcribed word by word,

24
00:01:33.525 --> 00:01:35.675
with who is speaking.

25
00:01:36.845 --> 00:01:38.825
And then the button that matters.

26
00:01:38.865 --> 00:01:41.155
Buscar ya. Search now.

27
00:01:42.293 --> 00:01:45.493
A two-hour interview is read in a little over a minute.

28
00:01:45.533 --> 00:01:48.723
The first Scholaris needed hours.

29
00:02:00.483 --> 00:02:01.713
Open the comedia.

30
00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:11.593
Up here is the folio. Not the number your PDF viewer shows,

31
00:02:11.633 --> 00:02:13.023
but the one printed on the paper.

32
00:02:13.063 --> 00:02:15.883
This is the eleventh page of the file.

33
00:02:15.923 --> 00:02:20.913
The book calls it five, and that is the number a footnote needs.

34
00:02:21.443 --> 00:02:24.273
On the left, the page as it was printed.

35
00:02:24.313 --> 00:02:29.763
On the right, its text, read faithfully, with the old spellings intact.

36
00:02:31.243 --> 00:02:35.673
On seventeenth-century print, it makes about ten times fewer character

37
00:02:35.713 --> 00:02:37.953
errors than the first Scholaris.

38
00:02:38.743 --> 00:02:42.453
And everything the machine did is open to inspection.

39
00:02:42.493 --> 00:02:46.663
Where each piece of data came from, how each folio was worked out,

40
00:02:46.703 --> 00:02:49.473
how sure it is, and what the reading cost.

41
00:03:00.143 --> 00:03:03.643
Type a folio, roman numerals included, and you are there.

42
00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:07.963
Where the book prints no number, Scholaris doesn't invent one.

43
00:03:08.003 --> 00:03:10.033
It says so, in brackets.

44
00:03:13.263 --> 00:03:18.303
Images are read too. Every figure gets a description you can search,

45
00:03:18.343 --> 00:03:22.493
marked for what it is: a description, never a citation.

46
00:03:29.303 --> 00:03:32.253
Books have pages. Interviews have seconds.

47
00:03:36.625 --> 00:03:41.535
This is Julio Cortázar in 1977, two hours on Spanish television

48
00:03:41.575 --> 00:03:46.035
with Joaquín Soler Serrano. Every word is anchored to its instant,

49
00:03:46.075 --> 00:03:49.285
and every turn to the person speaking.

50
00:03:50.725 --> 00:03:52.395
Alongside it, what the camera sees.

51
00:03:52.435 --> 00:03:57.265
Every change of scene, described, and searchable as well.

52
00:03:57.895 --> 00:04:01.385
Let's go to the moment he talks about Charlie Parker.

53
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.060
And then… when I read the biography of Charlie Parker,

54
00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:12.260
whom I admired immensely as a musician,

55
00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:16.060
it turned out that things he had done in his life, which later appear in the story,

56
00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:20.605
matched exactly the idea I had been looking for, and could not find.

57
00:04:22.375 --> 00:04:24.595
Select the sentence, and it becomes a citation.

58
00:04:24.635 --> 00:04:28.365
The interview, the year, the exact second.

59
00:04:30.395 --> 00:04:32.985
And it keeps playing while we go on looking.

60
00:04:40.275 --> 00:04:41.775
One search, across everything.

61
00:04:41.815 --> 00:04:45.445
Cortázar, Charlie Parker, jazz.

62
00:04:46.815 --> 00:04:49.495
The interview, at one hour and eight minutes.

63
00:04:49.535 --> 00:04:53.855
And the story itself, El perseguidor, page sixteen.

64
00:04:53.895 --> 00:04:59.175
A page and a second, side by side, as if they were the same kind of thing.

65
00:04:59.215 --> 00:05:00.665
Here, they are.

66
00:05:04.195 --> 00:05:05.875
It reads the way you write, too.

67
00:05:05.915 --> 00:05:11.885
Search in today's Spanish, and it finds Lope in the spelling of 1700.

68
00:05:13.395 --> 00:05:15.995
Exact words, meaning and the image of the page,

69
00:05:16.035 --> 00:05:20.565
combined, then reranked. Over one hundred and eighty-three test queries,

70
00:05:20.605 --> 00:05:24.465
the ranking scores 0.89 nDCG.

71
00:05:29.655 --> 00:05:35.665
Or ask. Why did Cortázar write El perseguidor thinking of Charlie Parker?

72
00:05:37.995 --> 00:05:41.415
The answer is short, and every sentence carries its note.

73
00:05:41.455 --> 00:05:44.755
No citation comes from the language model.

74
00:05:44.795 --> 00:05:49.745
Each one is checked against its passage before it reaches you.

75
00:05:50.765 --> 00:05:53.825
Click a note, and you are at the second he said it.

76
00:05:55.205 --> 00:05:58.275
Across forty-one test claims, invented citations:

77
00:05:58.315 --> 00:05:59.315
zero.

78
00:06:03.355 --> 00:06:05.285
Writing works the other way round.

79
00:06:09.895 --> 00:06:11.705
Paste a paragraph you wrote from memory.

80
00:06:11.745 --> 00:06:15.725
Scholaris looks for each claim in your library,

81
00:06:15.765 --> 00:06:20.275
and proposes a citation only where a passage supports the claim.

82
00:06:25.225 --> 00:06:29.925
You accept, or you don't. Then copy the reference in your journal's style,

83
00:06:29.965 --> 00:06:33.315
or take the whole text out as a Word document.

84
00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:50.220
A well-read library shows relations that no single book wrote down.

85
00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:54.350
Charlie Parker leads to Johnny Carter, the saxophonist Cortázar made

86
00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:58.220
of him, and to El perseguidor, the story he lives in.

87
00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:01.840
Every line is anchored to the page, or the second,

88
00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:03.590
it comes from.

89
00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:10.020
And the concept map gathers passages that say similar things,

90
00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:11.470
wherever they come from.

91
00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:26.450
A collection can be shared. With a colleague, by invitation,

92
00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:29.410
or with anyone, through a read-only link.

93
00:07:38.973 --> 00:07:43.533
It isn't only for people. Agents can use the same library,

94
00:07:43.573 --> 00:07:47.493
through an open API or an MCP server, under the same rule:

95
00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:50.043
cite only what Scholaris returns.

96
00:07:57.001 --> 00:08:00.601
The same app also runs on your own computer, with your library

97
00:08:00.641 --> 00:08:04.191
on your own disk. And what it reads becomes SPDF,

98
00:08:04.231 --> 00:08:08.601
an open format. Your library is yours to take.

99
00:08:18.686 --> 00:08:22.496
Scholaris won't write your essay, and won't decide which quotation

100
00:08:22.536 --> 00:08:26.126
is the right one. It takes you to the place.

101
00:08:26.166 --> 00:08:28.476
What you make of it is yours.

102
00:08:31.354 --> 00:08:37.784
Scholaris. Made by José Luis Saorín Ferrer, in Santa Cruz de Tenerife.
